# THE STRUCTURAL PACKET'S CENTREPIECE (lead ruling ST-002/ST-003, FR-008).
#
# The escalated class: "a hardening mechanism bound by hand to the site where a
# defect was reported, instead of derived from the members the module or
# artifact set actually contains." Four fixes in this module are the same shape:
#
#   D-097  `_dict_records` applied to ONE of the two filing doors  -> D-128
#   D-098  `_load_json` hardened in two of its three copies        -> D-130
#   D-103  `_save_json` given a unique sidecar in one of its copies
#   D-119  the cycle readers (closed by GUARDED_CYCLE_READERS)
#
# Each was fixed AT THE REPORTED SITE and left live copies behind. This log is
# the guard that makes the fifth copy unrepresentable.
#
# WHAT WAS RED AT ab5a430. The three rules were written first and run against
# the unfixed tree. They reported seven members, not the one the defect report
# had found:
#
#   unguarded document loads:
#     forge_spec.py::_load_json:32                       <- D-130, the known one
#     foundry_handoff.py::foundry_spec_hash:174          <- found by the scan
#     foundry_validate.py::foundry_validate_castings:104 <- found by the scan
#     foundry_validate.py::foundry_validate_castings:129 <- found by the scan
#     validation.py::validate_report:113                 <- found by the scan
#   unlocked artifact renames:
#     forge_spec.py::_save_json:38                       <- D-103's shape
#     intent_coverage.py::_save_json_atomic:98           <- found by the scan
#   raw ledger iterations:
#     foundry_orchestrator.py::foundry_sync_defects:3631,3644,3796  <- D-128
#     foundry_orchestrator.py::foundry_mark_defect_fixed:3260,3357
#
# Every one of the four the hand search missed is reachable over MCP:
# Foundry-Spec-Hash, Foundry-Validate-Castings and Validate-Report are all in
# server.py's _DISPATCH, and intent_coverage's unlocked writer writes
# castings/manifest.json, which two other tools read concurrently. That the
# scan found four more than the report is the argument for the scan.
#
# THE ALLOW-LIST IS EMPTY. This is not "the guarded primitives are enrolled
# here" -- there is nothing to enrol. `_read_document` carries its try/except
# in its own body and `_save_json` sits in a module that owns an flock
# discipline, so both PASS THE RULES ON THEIR OWN MERITS. A name allow-list is
# exactly the mechanism D-066 caught one level up; adding one would have
# committed the escalated class inside the guard meant to close it.
#
# THE TABLE BELOW is the forward claim, which is the one that matters: today's
# copies being gone is what the four prior fixes each achieved. Five FRESH
# copies -- verbatim reproductions of the shapes D-097, D-098 and D-103 closed
# -- are planted in modules none of those fixes touched, including the package
# ROOT (server.py's position, which owns _DISPATCH) and two NON-tools
# subpackages. All five are named. Membership is derived over both axes, files
# and directories, so neither a new module nor a new subpackage has to be
# remembered anywhere.
#
# WHAT THESE RULES DO NOT BIND, stated so silence is not mistaken for coverage.
#
#   - CLOSED (D-137, this cycle). This log used to declare the first residual
#     out of scope: "read_text() raising UnicodeDecodeError before json.loads is
#     reached (foundry_spawn.py:1163,1448 catch json.JSONDecodeError alone) -- a
#     property of the TEXT READ, not of the document load". That framing was the
#     defect. The read and the decode are ONE operation, and calling them two
#     classes is how 14 sites across 7 modules sat inside a gap this very scan
#     reported as clean. The rule now resolves handler names to exception
#     classes and asks issubclass, so the two plants added below --
#     decode_only.py and oserror_decode.py -- are named rather than accepted.
#
#   - OPEN, and genuinely a different class: a locked write primitive CALLED
#     from outside its lock (foundry.py's _save_json has six such callers).
#     Rule (b) binds a renaming module to owning a lock, not every call to
#     reaching the writer through it. foundry.py is casting 3's this cycle.
